  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UN Trust Fund for Indigenous Peoples</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Trust Fund on Indigenous Issues was established by General Assembly resolution 57/191 of 18 December 2002 and is intended to support the work of the Permanent Forum as follows:
To establish a voluntary fund for the Forum for the purpose of funding the implementation of recommendations made by the Forum through the Council, in line with paragraph 2 (a) of Council resolution 2000/22, as well as funding activities under its mandate, as defined in paragraphs 2 (b) and (c) of the same resolution;
The UN Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues (UNPFII) was established by the Economic and Social Council (Resolution E/2000/22) with the mandate to:
(a) Provide expert advice and recommendations on indigenous issues to the Council, as well as to programmes, funds and agencies of the United Nations, through the Council;
(b) Raise awareness and promote the integration and coordination of activities relating to indigenous issues within the United Nations system; and
(c) Prepare and disseminate information on indigenous issues.
The substantive areas of the mandate of the Permanent Forum relate to culture, economic and social development, education, the environment, health and human rights. The Permanent Forum has the distinction of being specifically mentioned in the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples: The United Nations, its bodies, including the Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues, and specialized agencies, including at the country level, and States shall promote respect for and full application of the provisions of this Declaration and follow up the effectiveness of this Declaration (Article 42). Within this framework, the Trust Fund supports the activities of the UN Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues, depending on availability of donor contributions.
The Indigenous</narrative>
